course: dictionary
lesson: 7421
title: Policy: a plan or set of rules
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A policy is a plan or set of rules that a group decides to follow. A school, a company or a government can all have one. The school's policy on phones tells everyone what is allowed and what is not.
explain: Policy is a noun, and its plural is policies. The words around it give clues. «Policies about bins, buses and prices are political decisions, so even people who say "I don't do politics" live with the results every day.» Here, policies are decisions that affect everyone, even people who think they are not interested.
explain: Policy has two other meanings. An insurance policy is a written agreement that pays out if something bad happens, like a fire at home. In the saying «Honesty is the best policy», it means that being honest is the wisest way to act.
fact: A policy is a plan or set of rules that a school, company or government decides to follow.
fact: Examples include a school's policy on bullying, a no-phones policy, and company policy.
fact: An insurance policy is a written agreement that the insurance company will pay if something bad happens.
fact: «Honesty is the best policy» means that acting honestly is the wisest way to behave.
fact: In maths, a policy can set a rule, such as a fine of $1 for each late book, which you can use to work out totals.
exercise: What is a policy? | *A plan or set of rules that a school, company or government decides to follow | A prize given for good work | A loud noise made by a bus | A kind of pen used only in offices
exercise: «Policies about bins, buses and prices are political decisions». What are these policies about? | Sun and rain | *Bins, buses and prices | Birds and trees | Books and pencils
exercise: In «Honesty is the best policy», what does policy mean? | A game played in the playground | *The wisest way to act | A written agreement with an insurance company | A type of food
test: An insurance policy is | A plan for school lunches | A kind of school bus | *A written agreement that pays out if something bad happens | A rule for football games
test: A school's no-phones policy means | Pupils must bring a phone every day | Phones are a subject you learn in class | *A rule the school has decided to follow about phones | Phones are a prize for good work
exam: Sam says a policy is just a boring word. Which reply is best? | A policy is a type of game played outdoors | *A policy is a plan or set of rules that people decide to follow, and it can affect school, shopping and insurance | A policy is a loud noise made by a bus | A policy is a kind of food you eat at lunch
